a curved line, as of a circle, especially one which does not include a point of inflection or cusp. {{smaller|It is by means of arcs of a circle that all angles are measured, the arc being described from the angular point as a center. In the higher}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller|mathematics the word ''arc'' is used to denote any angular quantity, even when greater than a whole circle: as, an ''arc'' of 750°. See ''angle''<sup>3</sup>.}}

'''2.''' In ''astron.'', a part of a circle traversed by the sun or other heavenly body; especially, the part passed over by a star between its rising and setting.
{{smaller block|<poem>The brighte sonne
The ''ark'' of his artificial day hath ronne
The fourthe part.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Man of Law’s Tale, l. 2.}}}}
'''3.''' In ''arch.'', an arch. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Turn ''arcs'' of triumph to a garden-gate.
{{right|''Pope'', Moral Essays, iv. 30.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Arc boutant''' (F.), a flying buttress.—'''Arc doubleau''' (F.), in ''arch.'', the main rib or arch-band which crosses a vault at right angles and separates adjoining bays from each other.—'''Arc formeret''' (F.), the arch which re-}}

