'''Arcadianism''' (är-kā′di-ạn-izm), ''n.'' [< ''Arcadian'' + ''-ism.''] Rustic or pastoral simplicity, especially as affected in literature; specifically, in Italian literature about the end of the seventeenth century, the affectation of classic simplicity.

'''Arcadic''' (är-kā′dik), ''a.'' [< L. ''Arcadicus'', < Gr. Ἀρκαδικός.] Same as ''Arcadian.''—'''Arcadic poetry,''' {{smaller|pastoral poetry.}}

'''arcana,''' ''n.'' Plural of ''arcanum.''

'''arcane''' (är-kān′), ''a.'' [< L. ''arcanus'', hidden, < ''arcere'', shut up, ''arca'', a chest. Cf. ''arcanum.''] Hidden; secret. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The luminous genius who had illustrated the demonstrations of Euclid was penetrating into the ''arcane'' caverns of the cabalists.
{{right|''I. D’Israeli'', Amen. of Lit., II. 294.}}}}

'''arcanum''' (är-kā′num), ''n.''; pl. ''arcana'' (-nä). [L., neut. of ''arcanus'', hidden, closed, secret: see ''arcane.''] '''1.''' A secret; a mystery: generally used in the plural: as, the ''arcana'' of nature.
{{smaller block|The very ''Arcanum'' of pretending Religion in all Wars is, That something may be found out in which all men may have interest.
{{right|''Selden'', Table-Talk, p. 105.}}
Inquiries into the ''arcana'' of the Godhead.
{{right|''Warburton.''}}
The Arabs, with their usual activity, penetrated into these ''arcana'' of wealth.
{{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., i. 8.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''alchemy'', a supposed great secret of nature, which was to be discovered by alchemical means; the secret virtue of anything. Hence—'''3.''' A secret remedy reputed to be very efficacious; a marvelous elixir.—'''The great arcanum,''' {{smaller|the supposed art of transmuting metals.}}
{{smaller block|He told us stories of a Genoese jeweller, who had ''the greate arcanum'', and had made projection before him severall times.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Jan. 2, 1652.}}}}

