'''arcature''' (är′kạ̄-t̮ūr), ''n.'' [< ML. *''arcatura'', < ''arcata'': see ''arcade''.] In ''arch.'': (''a'') An arcade of small dimensions, such as a balustrade, formed by a series of little arches. {{smaller|In some medieval churches open arcatures were introduced beneath the cornices of the external walls, not only as an ornament, but to admit light above the vaulting to the roof-timbers.}}
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Arcature.—Cathedral of Peterborough, England.}}}}
(''b'') A blind arcade, used rather to decorate a wall-space, as beneath a row of windows or a cornice, than to meet a necessity of construction.

'''arc-cosecant''' (ärk-kō-sē′kant), ''n.'' In ''math.'', an angle regarded as a function of its cosecant.
